21
00:01:40,830 --> 00:01:46,510
So, let's say you're working on a piece of software, and you're writing a feature for that software.

22
00:01:46,510 --> 00:01:49,110
Maybe you're writing a search feature or something like that.

23
00:01:49,110 --> 00:01:56,590
Now, the reason you're writing this feature, the reason in fact you're writing all the features of your software, is to try and make that software better for the users.

24
00:01:56,600 --> 00:01:59,150
And that in essence, is managing a risk.

25
00:01:59,160 --> 00:02:11,510
What you're doing there is you're managing the risk that the users aren't going to use your software, and therefore your company's not going to make money through that software, and then maybe your company goes bust because of that.

26
00:02:11,510 --> 00:02:15,670
Assuming that is you're selling software, maybe like a SaaS or some kind of commercial software.

27
00:02:15,670 --> 00:02:26,790
And so, you can see there's a very straightforward relationship there between writing good features in the software and managing the risks of the business.

28
00:02:26,790 --> 00:02:30,510
This is the case across pretty much everything that goes on in software.

29
00:02:30,510 --> 00:02:39,110
To give another example, if you're writing unit tests, what you're trying to do really is to avoid implementation risk in the software, bugs creeping in.

30
00:02:39,110 --> 00:02:41,110
And again, that's going to affect users down the line.

31
00:02:41,120 --> 00:02:46,230
That might encourage people to move away from your software and use something else.

32
00:02:46,230 --> 00:02:57,949
Or might maybe if you're working for a bank, like I've worked for many banks, there could be serious consequences of bugs making it into production where money is moved to the wrong people or ends up in the wrong accounts or is counted up wrongly.

33
00:02:57,949 --> 00:03:04,190
And so, these implementation risks turn out to be whole operational risks for the bank.

34
00:03:04,190 --> 00:03:06,110
And these are things that banks like to measure and track.

35
00:03:06,110 --> 00:03:10,350
And so, really the book is an exploration of this whole idea about how actually,

36
00:03:10,360 --> 00:03:20,830
although we enter this industry as software developers wanting to write code and wanting to be good about our craft, there's this underlying balance that's this risk landscape at play.

37
00:03:20,830 --> 00:03:26,750
And all the time what you're doing is you're working to minimize risks or moving to positions on the risk landscape where

38
00:03:26,760 --> 00:03:33,752
your software is in a more favorable position with more favorable risk facing it. So, that cover it? Yeah, it does.

39
00:03:33,760 --> 00:03:44,990
