41
00:03:47,550 --> 00:03:55,270
So, I think I'm going to assume that most of your audience are really familiar with traditional software development.

42
00:03:55,270 --> 00:04:02,670
Maybe you you work in like a Scrum team or with Kanban or some kind of lean approach or DevOps.

43
00:04:02,670 --> 00:04:06,310
Now, those approaches, sometimes they work, sometimes they don't.

44
00:04:06,310 --> 00:04:08,310
Often they're great and they work well.

45
00:04:08,310 --> 00:04:16,509
But what you have to remember is that in all those cases, you are actually just trying to manage risk.

46
00:04:16,509 --> 00:04:24,190
So, if I'm using Scrum and trying to limit my work to a couple of weeks work or having reviews and retrospectives,

47
00:04:24,190 --> 00:04:28,390
all these activities are in the service of managing risk on the project.

48
00:04:28,390 --> 00:04:32,750
For example, like work in progress, having lots of work in progress turns out to be risky.

49
00:04:32,750 --> 00:04:34,390
That makes your life more complicated.

50
00:04:34,390 --> 00:04:40,430
It means that features turn up later for users and that leads into that feature risk that I was talking about.

51
00:04:40,430 --> 00:04:49,990
So, I guess the way to look at this is that yes, all the techniques and tools that we use in software development, they all still apply.

52
00:04:49,990 --> 00:04:59,390
But what I'm trying to do is suggest that the reasons why some tools work better than others in certain situations is because they turn out to be better at managing risks.

53
00:04:59,390 --> 00:05:09,350
And so, having that more holistic understanding of what you're doing and the risks that you face in your work will actually be better off and help you choose the right techniques.

54
00:05:09,360 --> 00:05:20,710
So, if I understand that's right, what you mean is that the current methodologies like Scrum and Kanban, they've been created to solve specific problems.

55
00:05:20,710 --> 00:05:24,790
Like Scrum for visibility, you have visibility for the next two weeks if that's your sprint cycle.

56
00:05:24,790 --> 00:05:32,110
And if it's Kanban, it's for flow to understand if flow is getting impeded or it's getting blocked by certain work items.

57
00:05:32,110 --> 00:05:39,710
And in this context, where do they fall short and how does the Risk-First framework solve for it?

58
00:05:39,710 --> 00:05:47,070
And also, if you can help me understand is the Risk-First entirely new framework or does it work with the existing frameworks like Scrum or Kanban?

59
00:05:47,080 --> 00:05:49,710
Or does it replace it?

60
00:05:49,710 --> 00:05:53,550
Okay, so that's a really good question, really insightful question.

61
00:05:53,550 --> 00:05:58,470
So, let's start What I want to do is introduce the audience to the idea of a risk framework.

62
00:05:58,470 --> 00:06:03,190
And a risk framework generally, from working in finance, we see lots of these.

63
00:06:03,190 --> 00:06:13,710
But, the idea of a risk framework is that you have a particular context or a particular domain you're working in, and you break down the risks in that domain, and you enumerate what they are.

64
00:06:13,710 --> 00:06:20,830
And then you have a number of techniques or practices or tools or controls that you're using to manage those risks.

65
00:06:20,840 --> 00:06:23,830
And so, there's an interplay between those things.

66
00:06:23,830 --> 00:06:29,270
There's the risks on the one side, and then there's the techniques and controls that you're using on the other side.

67
00:06:29,270 --> 00:06:38,950
And so, if you think about something like XP, for example, XP recommends a number of different practices like test-driven development and pair programming.

68
00:06:38,950 --> 00:06:43,950
And those are the things on the right-hand side, they're the controls, if you like, or the techniques.

69
00:06:43,950 --> 00:06:46,270
On the left-hand side, the risks.

70
00:06:46,270 --> 00:06:51,390
If you read the literature on XP, it doesn't really talk so much about the risks.

71
00:06:51,390 --> 00:06:53,230
But, the risks are still there.

72
00:06:53,230 --> 00:07:03,150
So, if I think about something like test-driven development, the idea of that is to reduce the number of bugs in my code, and it's also to make the code more malleable and easier to change,

73
00:07:03,150 --> 00:07:09,070
because if I make a change, then I run my tests again, and if the tests all still pass, I know that it was a successful change.

74
00:07:09,070 --> 00:07:10,990
So, that is managing a couple of risks there.

75
00:07:10,990 --> 00:07:21,909
So, the technique of test-driven development is managing the risk of introducing implementation risks as you're changing code, but it's also managing a complexity risk.

76
00:07:21,909 --> 00:07:32,110
It allows you to refactor your code more easily, and therefore keep a lid on complexity, cuz obviously refactoring is one of the ways in which we can reduce unnecessary complexity.

77
00:07:32,110 --> 00:07:40,750
So, actually going back to XP, so the Kent Beck who invented XP, the very first sentence in his book XP Explained is, "It's all about risk."

78
00:07:40,750 --> 00:07:45,030
And so, he was very aware of the idea that what he was doing with managing risk.

79
00:07:45,040 --> 00:07:53,710
But rather than explain what all those risks were, he sort of dove into just explaining what his techniques were for solving them.

80
00:07:53,710 --> 00:07:57,630
So it's like he was showing you the answers to the problem without showing you the questions.

81
00:07:57,630 --> 00:08:02,470
Which is kind of a weird approach in retrospect, but that's what everybody does.

82
00:08:02,470 --> 00:08:10,670
People are always telling you what their techniques are and telling you what they're doing to solve a problem, but a lot of the time they're not explaining the context of the problem and the things they're solving for.

83
00:08:10,670 --> 00:08:17,110
And so to answer the other half of your question is like, does this replace anything?

84
00:08:17,110 --> 00:08:18,950
It's not really replacing anything.

85
00:08:18,950 --> 00:08:26,950
What I'm trying to do is backfill that part of the risk framework that was missing in books like XP explained,

86
00:08:26,950 --> 00:08:37,149
which is to say give people a list of the risks on that left-hand side of the risk framework ledger and say that these are the things that you care about when you're doing software development.

87
00:08:37,149 --> 00:08:43,950
And yes, you can carry on using your existing tools, but you need to be using the correct tools to manage the correct risks.

88
00:08:43,950 --> 00:08:54,710
I'm sure all our listeners know, but XP is extreme programming and that's one of the frameworks or the methodologies in addition to Scrum and Kanban that's used in software development.

89
00:08:54,710 --> 00:08:59,350
