90
00:08:59,350 --> 00:09:07,870
what you're saying is that the risk-first framework primarily provides context for why you're following certain practices for the methodology that you're following.

91
00:09:07,870 --> 00:09:17,070
So it is providing that overall context for the practices within extreme programming or Scrum or Kanban so that developers know those activities that

92
00:09:17,070 --> 00:09:21,911
they're performing, what risk does it actually reduce? Have I got that right?

93
00:09:21,911 --> 00:09:23,670
Yeah, that's exactly it.

94
00:09:23,670 --> 00:09:28,270
As I said, I've broken down like a taxonomy of different risks that we face on software projects.

95
00:09:28,280 --> 00:09:35,750
And then in the book I expand on these and I explain what they are and give examples of each and the tools that you can use.

96
00:09:35,750 --> 00:09:46,790
On the website I actually go into more detail and I talk about all these different development methodologies we discussed and I talk about each of the practices and how they relate to the risks and which risks apply to which practices and stuff.

97
00:09:46,790 --> 00:09:51,070
So, I'm really trying to create a rich network of what works with what else.

98
00:09:51,080 --> 00:09:54,791
But, yes, I think you've nailed it there. Yeah.

99
00:09:54,791 --> 00:10:04,190
Maybe double-clicking on it a bit more to understand if a team decides to implement the risk-first approach, what does that actual workflow look like?

100
00:10:04,190 --> 00:10:06,430
Say they want to start implementing it from Monday morning.

101
00:10:06,440 --> 00:10:08,110
What would that look like?

102
00:10:08,110 --> 00:10:11,510
Can you walk me through that?

103
00:10:11,510 --> 00:10:20,350
Yeah, so I think what I've been trying to stress so far is that I'm not trying to tell people a completely new way of developing software.

104
00:10:20,350 --> 00:10:22,510
I'm not saying throw out Kanban, throw out Scrum.

105
00:10:22,510 --> 00:10:24,230
If they're working for you, that's great.

106
00:10:24,230 --> 00:10:26,350
You carry on using them.

107
00:10:26,350 --> 00:10:31,590
What I am trying to do is to try and show the reasons why they are working.

108
00:10:31,590 --> 00:10:38,070
So, if you're using Scrum, some certain Scrum practices, then the reason it's working is it's because it's is dressing these risks.

109
00:10:38,070 --> 00:10:48,190
But, you're right in the sense that what I am saying is that if you're working on a project, what you should do is pay attention to the risks.

110
00:10:48,190 --> 00:10:58,190
You should be looking at the context you're working in and try to break down the sort of risks you're facing in a project and looking at those and

111
00:10:58,190 --> 00:11:02,350
paying attention to those and you can start to think about you can weigh off different risks against each other.

112
00:11:02,360 --> 00:11:11,390
You can look at how one risk might be more important or less important to the success or the health of the project than another.

113
00:11:11,390 --> 00:11:16,511
And in the book, I dive into a lot of different techniques for this. Most of these will be familiar.

114
00:11:16,520 --> 00:11:22,750
One is, for example, looking at how you might handle different time scales.

115
00:11:22,750 --> 00:11:30,110
So, a risk that you face now might be discounted less than a risk that you face in the future.

116
00:11:30,110 --> 00:11:34,590
Or, if you have risks of different sizes or shapes, they might be handled differently.

117
00:11:34,590 --> 00:11:41,190
You can use feedback loops in your work to uncover new risks because a lot of risks stay hidden.

118
00:11:41,190 --> 00:11:42,950
You have to think about the risks you don't know.

119
00:11:42,950 --> 00:11:49,310
And then also within teams as well, you might have communication issues between different teammates or across the projects.

120
00:11:49,310 --> 00:11:51,070
You need to think about communication risks.

121
00:11:51,080 --> 00:11:54,910
You also need to think about the wider context as well.

122
00:11:54,910 --> 00:11:57,070
What context is the project running in?

123
00:11:57,070 --> 00:11:59,350
Is the company itself facing certain risks?

124
00:11:59,350 --> 00:12:03,710
Is the country that the company is operating in?

125
00:12:03,710 --> 00:12:05,470
How does that affect the context of your project?

126
00:12:05,470 --> 00:12:07,070
So there are various different ways of looking at this.

127
00:12:07,070 --> 00:12:15,750
And interestingly, when you start to think about like the backlog for example, if you have a bunch of work on your backlog,

128
00:12:15,750 --> 00:12:25,110
often it can be difficult to prioritize things on the backlog, but looking at those, if you reconsider the items on your backlog and just consider them as a list of different risks.

129
00:12:25,110 --> 00:12:29,670
Try and figure out, well, what is the risk that this backlog item represents?

130
00:12:29,670 --> 00:12:33,830
That can usually offer an insight as to what you should be doing next.

131
00:12:33,830 --> 00:12:43,110
And do you have any example or does any story come to mind where getting this perspective about risk first helped a team successfully or

132
00:12:43,110 --> 00:12:48,550
what difference did it make in their progress to completing a project or a sprint?

133
00:12:48,550 --> 00:12:52,510
So this is one of the things that I like to include a lot of both in the book and on the website.

134
00:12:52,520 --> 00:12:59,110
So I as I said, I've broken down software development into different types of risks.

135
00:12:59,110 --> 00:13:01,350
So I'm going to go through a few of these now.

136
00:13:01,350 --> 00:13:07,150
So one is that I look at feature risks, the risks that a feature is present or not present.

137
00:13:07,150 --> 00:13:14,030
And feature risks often break down into things like, do I have the right features for the people that are going to be using my software?

138
00:13:14,030 --> 00:13:18,350
Does the market value the features that I'm creating?

139
00:13:18,350 --> 00:13:20,910
Are these valuable enough to be worth something and worth doing?

140
00:13:20,910 --> 00:13:26,870
And implementation as well, am I implementing these features well enough that other people are going to be able to use them.

141
00:13:26,870 --> 00:13:29,110
So that's kind of the feature risk side of things.

142
00:13:29,110 --> 00:13:31,670
And then I also consider dependency risks.

143
00:13:31,670 --> 00:13:41,030
So, obviously when we're building software, you conclude things like libraries, but you also have dependencies on things like the internet or certain infrastructure being available.

144
00:13:41,030 --> 00:13:51,590
And so, dependency risks break down into things like funding, processes, staff, agency, deadlines and schedules, things like this, having enough time to do things.

145
00:13:51,600 --> 00:13:56,950
And obviously library dependencies, database dependencies, things like this.

146
00:13:56,960 --> 00:14:02,510
There's also things like model risks where you're talking to other people, communication risks, complexity.

147
00:14:02,510 --> 00:14:06,670
Your understanding of the world is like a model.

148
00:14:06,670 --> 00:14:12,350
And so, it's important that if you're making moves in the world and you're doing things that your model aligns with that.

149
00:14:12,350 --> 00:14:14,630
But just to dive in to a specific example, right?

150
00:14:14,630 --> 00:14:19,310
So, one of the things I talk about is reliability risk.

151
00:14:19,310 --> 00:14:25,230
Now, as software developers, this is something I think about quite a lot when I'm choosing a dependency, say like a library dependency.

152
00:14:25,230 --> 00:14:31,510
Is this going to be a reliable choice for my project or am I going to regret this a few months down the line?

153
00:14:31,510 --> 00:14:33,270
But reliability risk shows up everywhere.

154
00:14:33,270 --> 00:14:41,910
So, one of the examples I pick up on the website is in the 1970s, Ford created a car called the Pinto.

155
00:14:41,910 --> 00:14:53,110
And they put the fuel tank of the car just behind the rear axle of the car, which created this massive reliability risk because if the car got hit from behind, which is kind of a super common thing, the fuel tank might get punctured and explode.

156
00:14:53,110 --> 00:15:00,350
And this happened over and over again to Ford and they faced huge damages and there were injuries and deaths and public outrage.

157
00:15:00,360 --> 00:15:05,750
And so, all of this was caused by a reliability failure in their software.

158
00:15:05,760 --> 00:15:10,110
So, you can see that as a particular example of reliability risk.

159
00:15:10,110 --> 00:15:18,790
But we face face this stuff all the time in software development with components having odd edge cases where they become unreliable or

160
00:15:18,790 --> 00:15:24,670
people putting lines of code in code bases and then forgetting about them and it going wrong in production in some kind of unusual way.

161
00:15:24,670 --> 00:15:29,270
So, these all represent reliability risks within software.

162
00:15:29,270 --> 00:15:30,790
Hopefully, that sketches out roughly what I'm talking about here.

163
00:15:30,800 --> 00:15:41,630
Yes, and it does transition us nicely into the next section that I wanted to cover, which was double click into some of the risks that you call out on your site as well as in the book.

164
00:15:41,630 --> 00:15:49,270
But, before that, I just want us to go back to the methodology itself and ask a few questions around that.

165
00:15:49,270 --> 00:15:58,430
So, is there a specific ceremony or a check-in that keeps the team focused on risk reduction rather than just focusing on the risk when we adopt the risk-first framework?

166
00:15:58,440 --> 00:16:03,950
How do people keep risk on the top of their mind?

167
00:16:03,950 --> 00:16:10,630
So, I think probably by this point it's come clear that I'm not trying to force a new methodology down people's throats.

168
00:16:10,630 --> 00:16:19,430
However, what I am suggesting that they do is to observe the fact that risk is going to be at the heart of their project's success or failure.

169
00:16:19,430 --> 00:16:28,510
So, it would seem sensible to have like a regular session where you go and review the state of either your backlog or you go talk to customers or you review your environment.

170
00:16:28,510 --> 00:16:32,590
There's a hundred and one ways in which you can close the loop on what you're doing.

171
00:16:32,590 --> 00:16:41,790
So, every time you do something in the world, really, you should be seeking to check to find out if what you did actually had the impact that you hoped for.

172
00:16:41,790 --> 00:16:44,590
And this is all a risk reduction strategy, isn't it?

173
00:16:44,590 --> 00:16:53,990
Because if you don't close the loop on work you're doing and test it, then this opens you up to all sorts of hidden risks and things not working out the way you wanted.

174
00:16:53,990 --> 00:17:02,270
Your model, essentially, is going to be underspecified compared to reality and you're going to be out of sync with what's really going on.

175
00:17:02,270 --> 00:17:09,069
So, to answer your question again, I'm not proposing that people have a specific ceremony around this,

176
00:17:09,069 --> 00:17:15,470
but nevertheless, in the book I do propose a number of different processes and things that you can adopt.

