00:18:14,870 --> 00:18:21,630
And then you have a conversation where technology, business, ops together to agree on that priority.

190
00:18:21,630 --> 00:18:29,910
Yeah, but I think what I'm saying is that that priority will be based on the perceptions that those people have of the risks that they see.

191
00:18:29,910 --> 00:18:35,910
So, maybe the product owner has one view of what the riskiest item on the backlog is and he'll want to drive towards that.

192
00:18:35,920 --> 00:18:39,590
Whereas somebody else reviewing the same backlog might have a different view.

193
00:18:39,590 --> 00:18:43,430
And so, they would prioritize things differently.

194
00:18:43,430 --> 00:18:51,630
But, the priority I'm suggesting is actually formed by risk and that is the underlying metric that they're going to be trying to rank things on.

195
00:18:51,630 --> 00:18:54,990
I was going to give a quick example there because I'm not sure Yes, that would help.

196
00:18:54,990 --> 00:18:57,310
if that was very clear.

197
00:18:57,310 --> 00:19:02,590
So, one of the things that I see over and over again working in finance is that this is not that long ago.

198
00:19:02,590 --> 00:19:06,790
So, there was a development team and there's also like the business team.

199
00:19:06,790 --> 00:19:14,510
The business obviously in finance often is trying to get some new business, they're trying to win some customers or something like this.

200
00:19:14,510 --> 00:19:20,030
Whereas the developers, they're doing their best to fulfill the needs of the business.

201
00:19:20,030 --> 00:19:25,830
But, often, push will come to shove and the business people will say, "We need this out yesterday.

202
00:19:25,830 --> 00:19:27,510
We need this working right now."

203
00:19:27,510 --> 00:19:30,710
And the developers will suck their teeth and go, "Okay, all right, come on.

204
00:19:30,710 --> 00:19:32,750
We'll just hack something in for now and we'll set it up."

205
00:19:32,750 --> 00:19:38,110
Now, obviously, hopefully, you have time afterwards to set things right and keep your architecture straight.

206
00:19:38,110 --> 00:19:41,110
But, sometimes, this keeps on happening and you end up with technical debt.

207
00:19:41,110 --> 00:19:45,350
Well, I think we've all been in situations where there's been lots of technical debt.

208
00:19:45,350 --> 00:19:52,790
And the problem I see a lot of the time is that the developers will then go to the business and say, "Look, we've got all this technical debt.

209
00:19:52,790 --> 00:19:56,470
We've got all these crazy edge conditions that you've asked us for over the last few months.

210
00:19:56,470 --> 00:20:00,550
We've introduced them and the software's in a bad state now."

211
00:20:00,550 --> 00:20:04,230
And the business people will say, "Yeah, that's all great, but we need to do X, Y, and Z now.

212
00:20:04,230 --> 00:20:07,030
You're just going to have to suck it up."

213
00:20:07,030 --> 00:20:16,110
Now, at this point, this is kind of a failure of communications because what the developers should be doing is not saying, "Oh, we see technical debt.

214
00:20:16,120 --> 00:20:19,390
We need to stop what we're doing and fix it."

215
00:20:19,390 --> 00:20:27,030
What they should be doing to the business is saying, "There are risks here in continuing down this path.

216
00:20:27,030 --> 00:20:29,110
We see complexity risk all over this system.

217
00:20:29,110 --> 00:20:33,310
And if we continue to operate it like this, this is going to end up in an operational risk.

218
00:20:33,310 --> 00:20:42,150
And either customers will be upset or they'll be frustrated that something's not worked or there's going to be some massive crashes or

219
00:20:42,150 --> 00:20:51,950
there'll be some odd calculations happening and it's going to end up in a massive reputational risk for the firm or we're going to end up losing money and it'll be a financial risk."

220
00:20:51,950 --> 00:20:54,790
You can extrapolate and try and paint the picture in terms of risk.

221
00:20:54,790 --> 00:20:59,590
And if you're communicating throughout an organization,

222
00:20:59,590 --> 00:21:08,350
then actually explaining your position in terms of risk is a really powerful way of crossing different organizational boundaries from one part to another because

223
00:21:08,350 --> 00:21:14,430
you can start to talk in terms of the language of risk and then hopefully people will understand that language.

224
00:21:14,430 --> 00:21:20,550
Whereas if you just go in saying, "Oh, we got lots of duplication of functions and technical debt and the bill keeps failing."

225
00:21:20,550 --> 00:21:26,670
That's not going to land in quite the same way as painting the picture of why it is important to address these problems.

226
00:21:26,680 --> 00:21:29,230
Yeah, it does bring it to light.

227
00:21:29,230 --> 00:21:31,590
I have a related question.

228
00:21:31,590 --> 00:21:38,950
So, I worked in a department previously in in a bank and the department was market risk.

229
00:21:38,950 --> 00:21:47,390
And within market risk, the primary function of the department was to do various calculations and come up with a number and that number was value at risk.

230
00:21:47,390 --> 00:21:54,150
In terms of if the market conditions moved, this would be the value that would be at risk for the firm at that certain point of time.

231
00:21:54,150 --> 00:21:58,670
The probability of that risk happening was quantified.

232
00:21:58,670 --> 00:22:06,310
The challenge in software I see is that we are able to explain the risk, but we are not able to quantify it.

233
00:22:06,310 --> 00:22:16,150
So, we are not able to put a number saying that if you have a tech debt or technology debt, which is say of 100 items,

234
00:22:16,150 --> 00:22:20,870
that is translating into a dollar value of the risk that the firm is carrying.

235
00:22:20,870 --> 00:22:28,350
We are not able to do that and that is a challenge that business is not been able to understand how does that convert to a risk to the firm.

236
00:22:28,350 --> 00:22:30,310
Do you see that from that perspective?

237
00:22:30,310 --> 00:22:34,310
And if you don't, how does the risk first framework address that?

238
00:22:34,310 --> 00:22:39,070
Does it allow developers or the software team to quantify the risk?

239
00:22:39,070 --> 00:22:41,350
Yeah, that's a great question.

240
00:22:41,350 --> 00:22:45,390
So, I also worked in the market risk team in one of my earlier jobs as well.

241
00:22:45,390 --> 00:22:48,670
So, before I answer that though, I just wanted to pick up on another suggestion here.

242
00:22:48,670 --> 00:22:53,750
So, it's interesting, isn't it, that we worked in the market risk team.

243
00:22:53,750 --> 00:22:56,310
And the way banks tend to work is that Well,

244
00:22:56,310 --> 00:23:07,910
in fact, the way a lot of companies work is that they will have an organizational structure and the organizational structure of the company is often broken down into different risks.

245
00:23:07,910 --> 00:23:11,870
So, if you think of like the chief finance officer is really in charge of financial risk.

246
00:23:11,870 --> 00:23:15,670
And then the chief operational officer is in charge of operational risk.

247
00:23:15,670 --> 00:23:23,070
And then when you break it down into the risk department that we work with for, we have the market risk team and the credit risk team and the liquidity risk.

248
00:23:23,070 --> 00:23:28,150
So, you can sort of see how this works, this idea of a taxonomy of risk.

249
00:23:28,150 --> 00:23:32,951
That's written large in the organizational structure of most firms. So, yes.

250
00:23:32,951 --> 00:23:42,390
So, that is essentially what I'm trying to do with software development is to create that same taxonomy so that we can start thinking about it in the same way and breaking it down.

251
00:23:42,390 --> 00:23:50,350
But yeah, let's get on to the question you asked which was like, well, so the great thing about value at risk was that you have a dollar number,

252
00:23:50,350 --> 00:23:57,430
which is the amount of money you're likely to lose on a given day 99 days out of 100, less than that, assuming it's like a 1% VAR figure.

253
00:23:57,440 --> 00:24:10,230
Obviously, the problem with value risk one of the problems with value risk is that it led a lot of people into a false sense of security about how likely that was to be the case.

254
00:24:10,230 --> 00:24:16,510
And when financial disasters happen, it wouldn't be just one day in a 100 where you'd lose that amount of money.

255
00:24:16,510 --> 00:24:20,630
It'd be like four or five days on the trot where you'd lose that amount of money.

256
00:24:20,630 --> 00:24:25,870
And so, suddenly the idea of value at risk starts to look a bit shaky.

257
00:24:25,870 --> 00:24:29,310
It's based on some slightly faulty assumption Well, these are not faulty assumptions.

258
00:24:29,310 --> 00:24:31,190
These assumptions are very clear in the model.

259
00:24:31,190 --> 00:24:37,950
But if you're basing what you do on just a model and accepting those assumptions, it's kind of a bit dangerous.

260
00:24:37,950 --> 00:24:41,830
And that has led to a lot of problems in finance, hasn't it?

261
00:24:41,830 --> 00:24:45,070
So, you have to be very careful, I guess, is the point I'm making.

262
00:24:45,070 --> 00:24:47,310
You have to be very careful with adding a dollar value to risks.

263
00:24:47,310 --> 00:24:51,070
But getting back to the nub of the problem, right?

264
00:24:51,070 --> 00:24:52,870
What you're saying is that, okay, I'm presented with my backlog.

265
00:24:52,870 --> 00:24:58,150
I've decided that all these items on my backlog represent risks.

266
00:24:58,150 --> 00:25:09,830
I can figure out using the Risk First taxonomy or I can use my own taxonomy but I've worked out what kinds of risks they all are and I've worked out which risks I care about most in reducing.

267
00:25:09,830 --> 00:25:13,710
But then, okay, is it important to assign a dollar value to each of these?

268
00:25:13,710 --> 00:25:18,550
So, what I would suggest is that often it's not that important.

269
00:25:18,550 --> 00:25:25,551
What you really need to be able to do is to weigh up two risks in your hand and say I've got risk A and risk B. Which one is worse?

270
00:25:25,551 --> 00:25:27,830
Which one do I care about more?

271
00:25:27,830 --> 00:25:29,910
And often times it's kind of obvious.

272
00:25:29,910 --> 00:25:32,470
You can say to yourself, "Oh, this risk is huge.

273
00:25:32,470 --> 00:25:35,950
The risk of me running out of money at the end of the month is massive.

274
00:25:35,950 --> 00:25:44,150
I really need to sort that out rather than worrying about translating the website into French so I capture the French market or something like that."

275
00:25:44,150 --> 00:25:46,590
I don't know.

276
00:25:46,590 --> 00:25:50,630
But yes, often times you can just sort of weigh these things by eyeball and say this one's bigger than this one.

277
00:25:50,640 --> 00:25:58,470
So, the risk is kind of metric. You don't have to be completely exact about the values, but certainly ordering them is is very beneficial.

278
00:25:58,480 --> 00:26:00,870
Right.

279
00:26:00,870 --> 00:26:05,070
Let's now deep dive into some of the specifics of the Risk First framework.

280
00:26:05,070 --> 00:26:07,310
You've seen a lot of projects in finance and in open source.

281
00:26:07,320 --> 00:26:13,590
Do you have a story or can you paint me a picture of a project where a risk was ignored?

282
00:26:13,590 --> 00:26:16,310
What What were the early warning signs that the team missed?

283
00:26:16,320 --> 00:26:18,870
Absolutely.

284
00:26:18,870 --> 00:26:26,870
So, the example I'm thinking of is we ended up working in quite a large IT project in a financial services firm, let's just say.

285
00:26:26,870 --> 00:26:36,470
And my team was working on like a data access layer and it was communicating with a number of different other teams like infrastructure via their different layers.

286
00:26:36,470 --> 00:26:39,350
It was kind of a microservices kind of approach.

287
00:26:39,350 --> 00:26:43,070
So, each different team was running their own different layers.

288
00:26:43,070 --> 00:26:45,030
And it was a massive team.

289
00:26:45,030 --> 00:26:52,750
There was maybe 30 or 40 people working in the the entire project and maybe seven or eight people just in my team working on this data access layer.

290
00:26:52,760 --> 00:26:58,830
Taking data out of a database and then handing it on to another team for use in some calculations.

291
00:26:58,840 --> 00:27:10,350
What was weird about it was that after really sort of straining and pushing hard with with a seven people team to try and make this work,

292
00:27:10,350 --> 00:27:17,110
eventually we kind of just de-scoped the whole thing and we decided to write a library that could be used by any of the other teams.

293
00:27:17,120 --> 00:27:25,150
We got rid of the entire idea of using like a microservice and we just handed them a library where they could just call it and run it in their teams.

294
00:27:25,150 --> 00:27:26,870
And this was kind of a rewrite.

295
00:27:26,870 --> 00:27:31,470
Me and one other guy just decided to rewrite this thing.

296
00:27:31,470 --> 00:27:41,670
So, from a team of maybe a seven or eight people working for a whole year and building a thing that didn't quite work, we went to a team of two people working for three months building a thing that did work.

297
00:27:41,670 --> 00:27:43,590
And it was a completely different design.

298
00:27:43,600 --> 00:27:47,990
And I think sort of just to try and bring this back to risk first,

299
00:27:47,990 --> 00:27:57,550
I think what went wrong here was that the project was started with a big budget and lots of fanfare and the teams were stocked up with people,

300
00:27:57,550 --> 00:28:04,070
but it wasn't completely clear what everybody should be doing and the the communication lines were not great.

301
00:28:04,080 --> 00:28:07,510
There were really just too many people working on the different components.

302
00:28:07,520 --> 00:28:09,110
Does this make sense?

303
00:28:09,120 --> 00:28:10,910
Yes.

304
00:28:10,910 --> 00:28:15,872
So, I think you're calling out communication risk, maybe complexity risk. Yes. Yeah, exactly.

305
00:28:15,872 --> 00:28:25,790
Yeah, and a bit of agency risk as well where you have people who really need to be doing something and so they find work for themselves to do.

306
00:28:25,790 --> 00:28:28,670
And so, yeah, people were building stuff that perhaps didn't need to be built.

307
00:28:28,680 --> 00:28:39,470
I really understood agency risk because that's something that you see in large complex organizations where managers or developers act in their own interest rather than the firms.

308
00:28:39,480 --> 00:28:52,550
How does a risk first view help expose someone who is pushing for a feature or tool because it reduces that personal risk, so they might be doing it for their own visibility or for their own career progression.

309
00:28:52,550 --> 00:28:58,110
Even if it increases technology risk, they still do it, and we have seen that happen time and again in large organizations.

310
00:28:58,110 --> 00:29:01,230
How does a risk-first framework address this?

311
00:29:01,230 --> 00:29:05,910
So, I think really what risk-first is doing is like collecting butterflies.

312
00:29:05,910 --> 00:29:14,510
It's finding examples of these things and pinning them down and attaching names to them along with all the other risks that we face, all the ones we've discussed today,

313
00:29:14,510 --> 00:29:19,110
and just giving you that palette of different risks to talk about that affect your projects.

314
00:29:19,110 --> 00:29:30,270
So, in essence, what I'm doing is cataloging things that you and I have probably seen hundreds of times over, and just making sure it's written down and in in this sort of taxonomy view.

315
00:29:30,270 --> 00:29:32,670
So, I'm not really suggesting anything particular about it.

316
00:29:32,680 --> 00:29:38,510
There are a bunch of So, for example, on the website, I talk about how agency risk occurs,

317
00:29:38,510 --> 00:29:49,390
like you get goal misalignments where the goals of the client and the goals of the employee don't line up or a lack of trans- parency where people are just able to operate without oversight.

318
00:29:49,390 --> 00:29:57,110
And then there's conflicts of interest where certain members of staff have goals that are not aligned with the goal of the project or the company itself.

319
00:29:57,110 --> 00:30:03,990
And so, all of these things kind of roll up into this idea of agency risk where, you know,

320
00:30:03,990 --> 00:30:15,630
the idea of agency risk, the term agency risk comes from the idea of principal agent problem, which is where if you have some money and you want to invest it, then often you'll find an agent to help you with that, like a financial advisor.

321
00:30:15,640 --> 00:30:21,150
But, the problem, of course, is how do you trust that they're going to do a good job of that?

322
00:30:21,150 --> 00:30:32,790
So, yes, I'm kind of pointing these things out, but yes, obviously every time you have these risks, there are already ways people have found of trying to correct for them.

323
00:30:32,790 --> 00:30:34,390
So, I'm not suggesting anything new or novel here.

324
00:30:34,390 --> 00:30:39,150
What I'm doing is just explaining what people have done in the past.

325
00:30:39,150 --> 00:30:47,710
And so, things like having a contract can help with agency risk or having review processes, monitoring, you know, getting people to commit to estimates, this kind of thing.

326
00:30:47,710 --> 00:30:50,150
These all help with agency risk problems.

327
00:30:50,150 --> 00:30:53,310
But, yeah, again, you've heard of all these, right?

328
00:30:53,310 --> 00:30:55,190
These are all things that we've all used in the past before.

329
00:30:55,190 --> 00:31:07,270
Even something like time boxing work, you know, having short iterative cycles helps with the agency risk problem because then you're checking work every so often rather than letting it go on and on and on without oversight.

330
00:31:07,270 --> 00:31:10,190
All right.

331
00:31:10,190 --> 00:31:16,390
You're giving a terminology for the risks that are pre-existing and that we are all aware of or we have seen it sometime in our career.

332
00:31:16,390 --> 00:31:21,070
So, that developers who are new can understand what are the risks that they should look out for.

333
00:31:21,080 --> 00:31:24,511
Yes, exactly. Yeah.

334
00:31:24,511 --> 00:31:33,070
And so, essentially, so what I'm doing really is applying this sort of discipline of risk management to software development in general.

335
00:31:33,070 --> 00:31:44,590
So, that idea we just discussed of trying to figure out what the the palette of risks are that you're facing and then coming up with a set of controls or practices around around those risks.

336
00:31:44,590 --> 00:31:48,270
This is what risk management is and this is what a risk framework is.

337
00:31:48,270 --> 00:31:53,030
So, I talked about at the beginning about having the risks on one side of the ledger and then the practices on the other.

338
00:31:53,030 --> 00:31:58,070
And so, yes, I'm just applying that that discipline, that idea to software development.

339
00:31:58,070 --> 00:32:01,790
And the ledger itself, is that a concept of risk first framework?

340
00:32:01,790 --> 00:32:05,110
That is that an artifact which is maintained when you adopt risk first framework?

341
00:32:05,110 --> 00:32:09,670
So, how do you make these risks surface? How do you log it?

342
00:32:09,670 --> 00:32:11,990
How do you once you have identified it, what are the next steps?

343
00:32:12,000 --> 00:32:26,950
Yeah, so I'm using the term ledger there just to just to add a bit of like so I don't say the words risk framework repeatedly over and over again in your podcast and get people bored cuz otherwise it does get a bit one note.

344
00:32:26,950 --> 00:32:28,830
But, yes, so that's a good question though.

345
00:32:28,830 --> 00:32:30,751
The idea of where do we store this stuff? Where do we keep it?

346
00:32:30,760 --> 00:32:39,790
We've already talked about like the backlog, the idea that the backlog is in itself kind of like a list of all your risks you're facing.

347
00:32:39,790 --> 00:32:44,790
People often have like a dedicated risk register where they're keeping a list of risks.

348
00:32:44,790 --> 00:32:53,390
So, on various projects that I work on for FINOS, which as you said earlier, we're an open source software foundation, we're part of the Linux Foundation,

349
00:32:53,390 --> 00:32:59,310
it turns out that quite a few of our projects are all about building risk frameworks.

350
00:32:59,310 --> 00:33:09,590
And so, we often embody these in like GitHub projects in just either in markdown files or in like little microsites that explain what's going on.

351
00:33:09,590 --> 00:33:14,990
So, just to give one example, we've we've been building like an AI risk framework recently.

352
00:33:14,990 --> 00:33:25,551
So, if you're working in a finance firm and you want to deploy AI as part of a software system in that firm, it's like, "Well, okay, well, what are the risks of doing that? What do I need to think about?

353
00:33:25,551 --> 00:33:27,630
You know, what do I have to control for?"

354
00:33:27,640 --> 00:33:34,550
We break down all those risks and then we talk about all the controls that you might introduce to to manage those risks.

355
00:33:34,550 --> 00:33:36,590
To answer your question then, it happens in various different ways.

356
00:33:36,590 --> 00:33:39,790
We we see this happening in in a number of different ways.

357
00:33:39,790 --> 00:33:49,270
In like big organizations like the banks we've worked for, they will actually have a a department that it really in charge of the risk taxonomy at some high level that's keeping track of all the risks.

358
00:33:49,280 --> 00:33:56,590
Usually, there's a chief risk officer who kind of keeps a register of all these different risks that the bank runs.

359
00:33:56,590 --> 00:34:09,350
Oh, and then the other example I was thinking of the other day, even something like So, I I live in the UK, the UK government has a risk register where it keeps track of all the different risks facing, you know, the UK as a whole.

360
00:34:09,350 --> 00:34:16,190
And this is a document you can actually download from the UK government website and it has all sorts of things like violent storms or

361
00:34:16,190 --> 00:34:23,590
like economic collapse or like major fires or all sorts of risks that they worry about and

362
00:34:23,590 --> 00:34:30,350
they they sort of record them all in the risk register and try and assign like the impact cost and the likelihood of of these risks happening.

363
00:34:30,350 --> 00:34:33,990
You've written extensively about the intersection of AI and risk.

364
00:34:33,990 --> 00:34:42,310
What are the some of the biggest risks that you see companies taking on right now when they deploy AI to production?

365
00:34:42,310 --> 00:34:44,190
That's a big topic, isn't it?

366
00:34:44,190 --> 00:34:48,590
The problem with this topic topic is that it's incredibly fast-moving.

367
00:34:48,590 --> 00:34:51,190
So, you know, the state of the art is changing fairly constantly.

368
00:34:51,200 --> 00:34:56,750
And what's being done now is probably not what we'll be doing in a year's time.

369
00:34:56,750 --> 00:35:03,510
So, that risk framework that I was talking about, the AI risk framework that Finos has written, it had its second revision last summer.

370
00:35:03,510 --> 00:35:12,390
And even then, within about 3 months, we realized that there was a whole load of stuff about agentic AI that we hadn't included.

371
00:35:12,400 --> 00:35:15,990
That's something that's, you know, with all this open claw and everything else.

372
00:35:16,000 --> 00:35:18,670
This is all really current.

373
00:35:18,670 --> 00:35:21,590
We're sort of rushing to keep up with that in these projects.

374
00:35:21,600 --> 00:35:26,910
So, for example, on the operational side, we have things like hallucination.

375
00:35:26,920 --> 00:35:38,710
Right, we're all aware of the This hallucination risk has got less, I think, over the last year, but it's still the case that you can get AI systems to hallucinate, you know, crazy stuff and give you the wrong answers.

376
00:35:38,720 --> 00:35:49,670
And if we're back in the world of finance, and there's some kind of important financial system behind or, you know, involved with this AI, that could be a terrible cost right there.

377
00:35:49,680 --> 00:35:58,350
There's a really interesting example of, I think, I can't remember the name of the airline company, but they had an AI chatbot on their website.

378
00:35:58,350 --> 00:36:05,950
And somebody managed to had a conversation with the AI bot saying that a member of their family had died.

379
00:36:05,950 --> 00:36:10,430
And so, were they entitled to money back off the tickets that they'd bought?

380
00:36:10,430 --> 00:36:13,870
And the bot was like, yes, of course, you should definitely have money back.

381
00:36:13,870 --> 00:36:18,070
And so, person filled out the bots the bot the forms the bot asked it to fill out.

382
00:36:18,070 --> 00:36:21,510
And then the company declined the offer.

383
00:36:21,510 --> 00:36:26,750
And so, then the person actually sued the airline and won.

384
00:36:26,750 --> 00:36:30,990
Won damages because the bot had said it was allowed to have the money back.

385
00:36:30,990 --> 00:36:42,150
So, this is an example of like a hallucination problem, where the bot has hallucinated that the idea of someone being allowed a compensation, and then it actually coming to pass that they got the compensation.

386
00:36:42,150 --> 00:36:44,590
So, this stuff has real material impact.

387
00:36:44,590 --> 00:36:50,790
Things like bias and discrimination, or lack of explainability, and reputational risk, we talk about that.

388
00:36:50,790 --> 00:36:54,350
So, that's another good impact of the thing I've the example I've just given.

389
00:36:54,350 --> 00:36:57,030
Information being leaked.

390
00:36:57,030 --> 00:37:08,710
So, if you wire up your AI system to sensitive information sources, and then rely on the AI to be the gatekeeper, it could be easily like prompt injected to give out information that it's not supposed to give out.

391
00:37:08,710 --> 00:37:12,310
So, all of these risks are things we talk about on the website.

392
00:37:12,310 --> 00:37:16,190
And yes, in the book as well, I sort of dive into this stuff.

393
00:37:16,190 --> 00:37:23,030
I like to What I've tried to do at the at the end of the book is to try and apply the risk first framework to what we're seeing now,

394
00:37:23,030 --> 00:37:32,150
in terms of the world of AI software development, AI and the effect it's having on society as a whole.

395
00:37:32,150 --> 00:37:35,630
And although I can't really, like I haven't got a crystal ball, I can't tell you exactly what's going to happen.

396
00:37:35,640 --> 00:37:45,510
What I can tell you is that risk management is really our best tool in the box for managing through this change that

397
00:37:45,510 --> 00:37:51,670
we're seeing in the world of moving towards autonomous intelligent software systems.

398
00:37:51,670 --> 00:37:57,590
This is really the best hope that we have of doing this in a safe way, and managing the the the downsides that there are in AI.

399
00:37:57,600 --> 00:38:07,830
Continuing with that, do you see any specific risks emerging because of use of agentic AI, where AI agents are committing code to our repositories?

400
00:38:07,830 --> 00:38:10,990
And do you see any new risks emerging because of this?

401
00:38:11,000 --> 00:38:14,750
Yeah, I am sort of fascinated in this stuff at the moment.

402
00:38:14,750 --> 00:38:19,350
So, I'm trying to put together a little risk framework about exactly this.

403
00:38:19,350 --> 00:38:27,550
One of the biggest ones that I kind of been worrying about a lot recently is the idea that if you're faced with some software that's actually quite reliable.

404
00:38:27,550 --> 00:38:30,190
And we see this in like self-driving cars.

405
00:38:30,190 --> 00:38:34,510
If you have a system that works 99% of the time,

406
00:38:34,510 --> 00:38:39,470
then what happens is a process called vigilance minimization where

407
00:38:39,470 --> 00:38:46,590
people will slowly over time just assume that the system's going to work fine up until the point where it doesn't.

408
00:38:46,590 --> 00:38:53,670
So and that could quite easily apply to I'm not sure you're using as much AI in your work as I am now,

409
00:38:53,670 --> 00:39:01,590
but you know, if you're assuming that the AI is going to do a good job and it does 99% of the time, then there are always then eventually that's going to catch you out.

410
00:39:01,590 --> 00:39:05,950
And I think this is something that I worry about a lot these days.

411
00:39:05,950 --> 00:39:11,390
I I'm sort of thinking how can we build systems where we, you know, control for that.

412
00:39:11,390 --> 00:39:16,230
How do we make a system where vigilance doesn't get reduced over time?

413
00:39:16,240 --> 00:39:19,350
I'd be interested to see what you think about that.

414
00:39:19,350 --> 00:39:22,030
Yeah, like you said, it's a fast-evolving world.

415
00:39:22,030 --> 00:39:34,352
And I think the bank Well, at least the bank that I'm working in has taken the approach that everything's tightly governed around here because we just can't put any of our customer information or customers at risk. Right. Yeah.

416
00:39:34,360 --> 00:39:48,030
too fast. But at the same time, you must be worried that one of your competitors will steal a march on you and then do something amazing in the space and steal business, I guess, right?

417
00:39:48,030 --> 00:39:50,712
That's the flip side of that. Yes. Okay.

418
00:39:50,712 --> 00:39:56,590
Which is why Yeah, you want to keep a close watch and understand what's happening in that domain and that area.

419
00:39:56,600 --> 00:40:11,351
But move cautiously because the risk of getting things wrong far outweighs the risk of not being the first to roll out a new feature or a new product, I guess, using AI. I think you're right.

420
00:40:11,351 --> 00:40:18,630
I I think it will probably also depends on the size of the organization and how much they have to lose as well, don't it?

421
00:40:18,630 --> 00:40:22,510
you're working for one of the big tier one firms, that's much must be very much the mindset.

422
00:40:22,510 --> 00:40:29,270
Whereas if you're working for some small challenger bank, maybe that's not going to be the case and they're going to be thinking,

423
00:40:29,270 --> 00:40:36,830
we need to aggressively push into this technology and and try and do something that's going to capture the public imagination with it.

424
00:40:36,840 --> 00:40:40,230
Yes.

425
00:40:40,230 --> 00:40:46,751
Does that mean the risks differ based on factors like the size and size of the organization? Yeah, exactly.

426
00:40:46,751 --> 00:40:49,350
So, that's a a really good point, isn't it?

427
00:40:49,350 --> 00:40:56,510
So, going back to something like the AI governance risk framework, in order to build that,

428
00:40:56,510 --> 00:41:06,350
we collected together people who worked across the banking industry, worked for multiple different firms, and they all sort of collaborated on producing this list of risks.

429
00:41:06,350 --> 00:41:16,070
The good thing about that is, and this is something I mentioned in the book as well, is that the more people you have, the more different opinions you have, the better collection of risks you'll end up with.

430
00:41:16,070 --> 00:41:26,670
And that's kind of the reason why all these firms wanted to collaborate together, cuz they knew that they'd end up with a better result than if they any individual firm did it themselves. But yes, you're absolutely right.

431
00:41:26,670 --> 00:41:37,470
Um although they can agree on what the risks are, they're not going to agree on how important those risks are, the the size of the threat to them, you know?

432
00:41:37,480 --> 00:41:46,110
So, different firms are going to have different opinions about both the the impact of that risk on them, but also the probability of it happening.

433
00:41:46,110 --> 00:41:57,630
So, as a software developer, you need to be aware of the context because if you move from a big organization to a fintech, the importance you give to the same risk will be quite different.

434
00:41:57,640 --> 00:42:01,552
Yes. Yeah, exactly. Yeah.

435
00:42:01,552 --> 00:42:07,670
Related to the organization you're working in FinOps, banks usually,

436
00:42:07,670 --> 00:42:13,750
you know, the chief security officer in a bank is usually takes an approach or takes a view that we must control everything.

437
00:42:13,760 --> 00:42:19,670
How does FINOS help to change that perspective to we can collaborate safely?

438
00:42:19,680 --> 00:42:25,270
And how does the risk first framework help support those conversations?

439
00:42:25,280 --> 00:42:29,350
So, when you say the security office, you mean like cybersecurity, I presume?

440
00:42:29,360 --> 00:42:32,310
Yes. Yes. Yeah.

441
00:42:32,310 --> 00:42:34,430
So, this is a really good question.

442
00:42:34,430 --> 00:42:36,830
Within FINOS, I work on a project called common cloud controls.

443
00:42:36,840 --> 00:42:41,150
And as you can probably guess, common cloud controls is a risk framework.

444
00:42:41,150 --> 00:42:46,070
So, what we've done is we've taken all the commonly used cloud services.

445
00:42:46,070 --> 00:42:56,030
So, things like managed Kubernetes or object storage or relational databases, and we've broken them down into what they do, and then we've broken them and we've looked at the risks on those things.

446
00:42:56,030 --> 00:43:00,470
So, we've tried to do a risk analysis of the different services.

447
00:43:00,470 --> 00:43:08,390
And then we've started working out what the controls are that you need to run those services safely in a financial services organization.

448
00:43:08,390 --> 00:43:13,670
So, you think this is another example within FINOS where risk frameworks are useful.

449
00:43:13,670 --> 00:43:15,830
And there are a bunch of others that we might get into.

450
00:43:15,840 --> 00:43:27,830
So, your question really was like, why would a bank or a financial services firm, why would they want to use that rather than come up with their own internal approach to this?

451
00:43:27,830 --> 00:43:30,910
And there's a couple of reasons.

452
00:43:30,910 --> 00:43:40,110
So, the first reason is that maintaining like a this sort of suite of cybersecurity controls is kind of hard work.

453
00:43:40,110 --> 00:43:46,790
And it's also, if you can think about it, there are people in every different firm doing exactly the same thing.

454
00:43:46,790 --> 00:43:52,990
So, all of the big financial services firms in the city or your city or my city are doing exactly that same work.

455
00:43:52,990 --> 00:43:56,070
They're all building the same set of controls.

456
00:43:56,070 --> 00:44:00,070
And to a greater or lesser to extend, they're, you know, doing a better or worse job of it.

457
00:44:00,070 --> 00:44:07,470
And so, this is kind of like a basic argument for open source, really, because what we're saying is that stop building those controls on your own,

458
00:44:07,470 --> 00:44:16,390
build them together, and do a better job as a group than you would otherwise do individually.

459
00:44:16,390 --> 00:44:23,790
Now, proje- it turns out that getting financial services firms to engage in open source is very difficult, and that's probably a whole other podcast that we can get into.

460
00:44:23,790 --> 00:44:25,510
It's not as simple as I've made it sound.

461
00:44:25,510 --> 00:44:27,670
If if it was, we'd be done a long time ago.

462
00:44:27,680 --> 00:44:38,390
But yes, the the idea is that if they all come together and they collaborate on these things as a group, they will end up with something better than they would if they were just doing it on their own.

463
00:44:38,390 --> 00:44:42,310
And then also, the more people that get involved in this, the less work they have to do.

464
00:44:42,310 --> 00:44:45,310
It's sort of just divide and conquer essentially.

465
00:44:45,310 --> 00:44:50,990
And yeah, I think that is the basic argument for any open source.

466
00:44:50,990 --> 00:44:56,750
It's just a question of persuading the right people in firms to to allow it to happen, I think.

467
00:44:56,750 --> 00:45:01,070
And what it tends to boil down to is that or what it should boil down to is that

468
00:45:01,070 --> 00:45:11,910
where people are working on some kind of commodity work that is going to be repeated in lots of organizations, they should probably be doing that in an open source project.

469
00:45:11,910 --> 00:45:21,110
And where they're doing something that is generating alpha, where it's unique to their firm, that's where they should keep it in-house and keep it as the secret sauce.

470
00:45:21,110 --> 00:45:31,030
But I don't know about you, Bajesh, but I've over the course of my career, I've been to various different firms and done pretty much the same job at a number of different places.

471
00:45:31,030 --> 00:45:41,870
And I guess that's the bread and butter for a lot of people working in finance, and it's one of the reasons why all the financial services firms, you know, co-locate in the you know, the city, the financial districts in a city.

472
00:45:41,870 --> 00:45:44,590
It's so that the staff can move from one firm to another.

473
00:45:44,590 --> 00:45:51,630
But open source offers a much better way of sharing this knowledge between between firms than just having your staff moving around, I think.

474
00:45:51,630 --> 00:45:56,110
So that's the glittering prize of of like working on something like common cloud controls.

475
00:45:56,120 --> 00:45:58,910
Right.

476
00:45:58,910 --> 00:46:05,510
And I was just trying to overlay the risks that the categories of risks that you had mentioned earlier in terms of,

477
00:46:05,510 --> 00:46:11,350
you know, if you looked at using open source or building your own software,

478
00:46:11,350 --> 00:46:18,430
what risk would be would we be reducing if we used open risk and what risk would increase if we used open risk open source and similarly,

479
00:46:18,430 --> 00:46:25,670
if we built our own software, custom one, what risks would we be reducing and what would we be increasing? Can you help me with that?

480
00:46:25,680 --> 00:46:34,470
And again, this is another side of the the work I've been doing at FINOS is is building a risk framework for exactly that.

481
00:46:34,470 --> 00:46:37,270
So, try and catalog the different risks of open source.

482
00:46:37,280 --> 00:46:40,830
Before I go on there, there's kind of two sides to open source.

483
00:46:40,830 --> 00:46:46,150
There's open source consumption where you're pulling in libraries and using them in your organization.

484
00:46:46,150 --> 00:46:48,030
And then there's open source contribution.

485
00:46:48,030 --> 00:46:52,150
And so, they're kind of different things and they have different risk profiles.

486
00:46:52,150 --> 00:46:57,990
And it's almost the case that I don't really need to win an argument about open source consumption.

487
00:46:57,990 --> 00:47:00,110
Like every firm is using open source left, right, and center.

488
00:47:00,120 --> 00:47:09,910
There was some Red Hat's study recently where they said that like something like 90-something percent of every single piece of software is open source components.

489
00:47:09,910 --> 00:47:19,470
Certainly all the software I've built in my career has brought in open source dependencies and it's run on things like Linux and what have you. So, using open source is kind of a no-brainer.

490
00:47:19,470 --> 00:47:28,430
What they need to do when they're using open source is make sure they think about license compliance or supply chain security or inventorying their components.

491
00:47:28,430 --> 00:47:33,430
So, that's one side of things and it's surprising how often that goes wrong, but those risks at least are well known.

492
00:47:33,440 --> 00:47:43,670
So, on the contribution side, it's like, what are the risks of contributing to open source software and what are the risks of not contributing to open source software?

493
00:47:43,670 --> 00:47:47,630
So, that's the interesting part.

494
00:47:47,630 --> 00:47:53,070
So, if you're not contributing to open source software, then perhaps you're not able to steer the direction of the project.

495
00:47:53,070 --> 00:47:57,910
That's kind of a big thing for something like common cloud controls.

496
00:47:57,910 --> 00:48:05,870
If you're not contributing to open source software, then maybe you will not see the benefits of talent arriving at your firm.

497
00:48:05,870 --> 00:48:11,710
Often, having a good open source posture is very attractive to developers arriving at your organization.

498
00:48:11,710 --> 00:48:15,630
It's kind of like a mark of the quality of the development work going on in your firm.

499
00:48:15,630 --> 00:48:24,870
And one of the big ones is that if you don't allow staff to contribute to open source software, and then they find an issue in that software,

500
00:48:24,870 --> 00:48:33,550
an open source component that they want to use, what they'll do is they'll take a copy of that software, they'll stick it in their internal source control, and then they'll make the change locally,

501
00:48:33,550 --> 00:48:37,710
and then they'll support that from that day forwards forever.

502
00:48:37,710 --> 00:48:39,670
That's both expensive, so it's got financial risk associated with it.

503
00:48:39,680 --> 00:48:53,470
That developer is now wasting a lot of time integrating upstream dependencies, but it's also a security risk because the chances are they're not keeping that branch up to date with what's going on in the public repo, either.

504
00:48:53,470 --> 00:48:59,350
So, there's a whole bunch of risks there if you don't contribute to open source software.

505
00:48:59,350 --> 00:49:01,790
But then there's also a whole bunch of risks if you do.

506
00:49:01,790 --> 00:49:11,390
So, one of the key ones that firms really worry about in regulated industries like finance is that

507
00:49:11,390 --> 00:49:20,270
some, you know, hapless developer will accidentally share some private client information in a GitHub repository, or they'll paste it onto Facebook or or whatever.

508
00:49:20,270 --> 00:49:31,670
And you usually the firewall policies in an organization will prevent access to these sites and prevent people from you know, doing HTTP post requests and things like this in order to minimize that risk.

509
00:49:31,670 --> 00:49:41,070
So, there's this huge worry around accidentally posting code that you didn't want to release,

510
00:49:41,070 --> 00:49:48,750
or PII data, or materially sensitive information, company secrets, secret keys, server details, all this sort of stuff.

511
00:49:48,750 --> 00:49:51,270
You've got to be careful with all that.

512
00:49:51,270 --> 00:49:59,230
And so, just opening the floodgates and allowing any employee to use their work computer to contribute to open source is a huge risk.

513
00:49:59,230 --> 00:50:03,270
And so, Finos actually provides tools like Git Proxy,

514
00:50:03,270 --> 00:50:10,350
which is like a proxy that sits between your staff computer and then GitHub or

515
00:50:10,350 --> 00:50:16,470
GitLab and allows for a like a four-eyed review to occur to anything you want to push to the open source project.

516
00:50:16,470 --> 00:50:20,070
So, I guess what I'm saying is that you don't have to just accept these risks as they are.

517
00:50:20,070 --> 00:50:30,111
You can actually use tools to manage those risks and and make that open source contribution option much more acceptable from a risk profile point of view. Does that make sense?

518
00:50:30,120 --> 00:50:32,591
Yes, it does. Thanks.

519
00:50:32,591 --> 00:50:34,550
I think we've covered a lot of ground over here.

520
00:50:34,550 --> 00:50:38,191
Yeah, we've used the word risk about a million times as well, haven't we? So.

521
00:50:38,200 --> 00:50:47,070
You know, as we conclude, my final question would be, what's the one thing you hope listeners will walk away thinking about after listening to this show?

522
00:50:47,070 --> 00:50:54,110
I think the final thing that I would like people to take away is kind of the central thesis of the book that all work is risk management. It doesn't matter what it is, right?

523
00:50:54,110 --> 00:51:02,830
If you're a farmer and you're selling crops, you're you're like, this is managing the risk that people go hungry and and have nothing to eat.

524
00:51:02,830 --> 00:51:08,390
And if you're uh I don't know, think of another example, but this applies to any kind of work you're doing.

525
00:51:08,390 --> 00:51:16,550
I once had a friend who's a lawyer and I was explaining the concept of the book to them and they were like, oh yeah, no, law is all risk management as well.

526
00:51:16,550 --> 00:51:23,510
So, I guess what I'm saying to the listeners is maybe go away and think about what you're doing and test this theory.

527
00:51:23,510 --> 00:51:25,270
Is all the work you're doing risk management?

528
00:51:25,270 --> 00:51:27,710
Can you view it through a risk management lens?

529
00:51:27,710 --> 00:51:32,711
And does that actually help you decide what to work on better? For me, it certainly does.

530
00:51:32,711 --> 00:51:39,431
I find I use this lens all the time for deciding how best to proceed on projects. Excellent.

531
00:51:39,431 --> 00:51:40,950
Rob, thank you for coming on the show.

532
00:51:40,950 --> 00:51:42,830
It's been a real pleasure.

